{
  "summary": "Refactors the logging macros in util/log.h by consolidating duplicated macro logic into a unified LOG_EMIT macro that takes compile-time options. Replaces opaque macro expansion errors with static assertions that validate category arguments at compile time, and replaces the NO_RATE_LIMIT tag with an explicit ratelimit option. Avoids message formatting overhead when logging is completely disabled while explicitly preserving argument evaluation for Info and higher log levels.",
  "problem": "Logging macros duplicated logic and had undocumented inconsistencies. Misusing category arguments (such as passing a category to LogInfo or omitting one from LogDebug) produced confusing macro expansion syntax errors. In addition, format strings were formatted even when logging was completely disabled, and rate-limiting hints were stored in buffered log entries after rate limiting had already occurred.",
  "evidence": [
    "Compile-time errors for mismatched category arguments now produce clear messages via static_assert ('Missing required category argument for Debug/Trace logging call' and 'Cannot pass category argument to Info/Warning/Error logging call') in util/log.h.",
    "Commit f5737f0478 cites PR #34374 review discussion noting that evaluating arguments even when logging is disabled at Info or higher is important to avoid unintended side effects.",
    "Author notes in commit 6292d61b55 that skipping formatting when logging is disabled avoids unnecessary strprintf calls in fuzzing and kernel applications."
  ],
